From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] viogpu3d: refresh rates up to 240 Hz

The driver offered one 60 Hz timing and ran its vblank timer at 60 Hz, so Windows composed at most 60 frames per
second even where the display behind it (a SPICE client on a faster monitor) shows more. Offer every mode at 60, 75,
90, 100, 120, 144, 165, 180 and 240 Hz (target and monitor modes; 60 Hz stays preferred), and run the vblank timer at
the rate of the target mode Windows commits. QEMU's EDID limits the monitor to 50-125 Hz and 30-160 kHz, and Windows
drops the modes outside it: widen the range limits descriptor. DescribeAllocation reported 60 Hz for every
allocation, and Windows flips only between primaries made for the current mode: at any other rate DWM stopped
presenting. Report the committed rate.
---
diff --git a/viogpu/viogpu3d/viogpu_allocation.cpp b/viogpu/viogpu3d/viogpu_allocation.cpp
index 515c4c4..349ed37 100644
--- a/viogpu/viogpu3d/viogpu_allocation.cpp
+++ b/viogpu/viogpu3d/viogpu_allocation.cpp
@@ -307,8 +307,10 @@ NTSTATUS VioGpuAllocation::DescribeAllocation(DXGKARG_DESCRIBEALLOCATION *pDescr
     pDescribeAllocation->MultisampleMethod.NumQualityLevels = 2;
     pDescribeAllocation->MultisampleMethod.NumSamples = 2;
 
-    pDescribeAllocation->RefreshRate.Numerator = 148500000;
-    pDescribeAllocation->RefreshRate.Denominator = 2475000;
+    // The committed mode's: Windows flips only between primaries made for the current mode, and DWM stopped
+    // presenting at any other rate when this was always 60 Hz
+    pDescribeAllocation->RefreshRate.Numerator = m_adapter->vidpn.GetRefreshRate();
+    pDescribeAllocation->RefreshRate.Denominator = 1;
 
     return STATUS_SUCCESS;
 };
